#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124288 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a568fc63-e81c-5210-93f0-bcbd429a9e40" :question/math-academy-id "e-6678" :question/problem "A biologist observed that the growth of the number of fish in a lake is directly proportional to the difference between the lake’s maximum fish population and the current population. Let $P(t)$ be the fish population, measured in number of fish, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum fish population that the lake can support is $200$ fish, which differential equation could be used to model the lake’s fish population? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P)\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\na = 200\\,\\textrm{fish}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d603efdd-6e27-5e14-a106-f9fc4a182403" :question/math-academy-id "e-6679" :question/problem "In an aquarium, there are initially $20$ fish. After $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50$. Assuming inhibited growth, calculate the number of fish after $100$ weeks if the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $90$ fish." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of fish $N(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the aquarium's maximum capacity and the current number of fish. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k\\left(a-N\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 90$ fish, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(90-N)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dN}{90 - N} &= kdt \\\\\n\\int \\frac{dN}{90 - N} &= \\int kdt \\\\\n- \\ln \\mid 90 - N \\mid &= kt + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $90 - N > 0$ for all\n\n$$\nt \\ge 0\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $N(t)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90 - N &= e^{-kt - C} \\\\\n90 - N &= e^{-C}e^{-kt} \\\\\n90 - N &= Ke^{-kt} \\\\\nN &= 90 - Ke^{-kt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial number of fish was $20$. So, we apply the initial condition $N(0)=20$, and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(0) &= 20 \\\\\n90 - Ke^{0} &= 20 \\\\\n90 - K &= 20 \\\\\nK &= 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $N(t)$:\n\n$$\nN(t)=90 - 70 e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50$. So, applying the condition $N(30)=50$, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(30) &= 50 \\\\\n90 - 70e^{-30k} &= 50 \\\\\n40 &= 70e^{-30k} \\\\\ne^{-30k} &= \\frac{4}{7} \\\\\n- 30k &= \\ln (\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{1}{30}\\ln (\\frac{4}{7})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nN(t)=90-70e^{(t/30)\\,\\ln\\left(4/7\\right)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of fish after $t=100$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(100) &= 90 - 70e^{(100/30)\\ln (4/7)} \\\\\n&= 79.161\\ldots  \\\\\n&\\approx 79\\,\\mathrm{fish}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124291 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d178c83a-b8af-5d6a-bf40-fcfb7506feeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6686" :question/problem "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n4. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ for all\n\n$$\nt \\ge 0\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4(60-y)\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4$, and $a=60$. Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y \\lt 60$ for all\n\n$$\nt \\ge 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd57bce4-bf4d-5488-ada6-93439471fb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5y^{2} - 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121106} {:answer/id #uuid "1f00b80a-9440-52ad-bb3e-9a6954010114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5 - 400y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121107} {:answer/id #uuid "34a003e9-7090-500a-bcd5-5a07bde1c2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121108} {:answer/id #uuid "35a91da3-7b8e-54e8-8314-4340d804eddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5y - 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121109} {:answer/id #uuid "6623a9f2-ffe7-561f-9984-2a59cbb33aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a003e9-7090-500a-bcd5-5a07bde1c2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4164cc7-8b43-5b53-b800-89f7a6270478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "092a8c01-7fc1-532c-ba20-9cb4c823a717" :question/math-academy-id "q-106662" :question/problem "Given that $y < 80$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Factor $400-5y=5(80-y)$. Thus $y^{\\prime}=400-5y$ is inhibited growth with positive constant $k=5$ and equilibrium $80$. Since $y<80$, the solution increases toward $80$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd2b0d2f-278e-5f3a-872b-4c55db1063e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3(250 - y), y(0) = 150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121112} {:answer/id #uuid "d93318fe-144b-54f6-ad46-9f78e39caca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-3(y + 250), y(0) = 100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121113} {:answer/id #uuid "118e1393-433d-5237-b2ec-5ca7c22512e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3(250 - y), y(0) = 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121114} {:answer/id #uuid "33cf5133-7c8a-5eac-b8a8-3fa513d8038e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3y(y - 250), y(0) = 50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121115} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea36dc2-bfcf-5d09-b596-d2ace383757e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3(y - 250), y(0) = 200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd2b0d2f-278e-5f3a-872b-4c55db1063e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3(250 - y), y(0) = 150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121112} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ee9563-8785-53ef-b91d-f1c369b24a3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f97edbb-38b9-5a3e-9db9-d7a29ce3a4af" :question/math-academy-id "q-106663" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The required form is $y^{\\prime}=k(a-y)$ with $k>0$ and an initial value below $a$. The equation $y^{\\prime}=3(250-y)$ has equilibrium $250$, and $y(0)=150<250$. Its solution is $y(t)=250-100e^{-3t}$, which increases toward $250$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b993a9-4262-511e-b44b-5bfc2baded85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121292} {:answer/id #uuid "92ea1b9a-48f6-5f5a-970a-360e074d7cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$21$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121293} {:answer/id #uuid "ded23566-a047-57a7-a52e-d55db3b390cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$17$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121294} {:answer/id #uuid "6205c386-c28f-5b02-994f-a140a082b1b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121295} {:answer/id #uuid "6c271daf-0958-59ce-9182-e17b45a13ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c271daf-0958-59ce-9182-e17b45a13ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121296} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0cf405c-ac0d-51e2-8a17-9653fef2ee4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "754ac309-f23b-53d4-8c3a-d4c7dd3dd38f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15370" :question/problem "A University Campus has a total of $1500$ students.\nAt the beginning of the new semester at the University, flu affected $300$ students. After $10$ days, the number of students affected by the flu increases to $500$. To the nearest day, how long does it take for the flu-stricken campus population to reach $800$ students?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of students affected by the flu, $P(t)$, increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The model is $P^{\\prime}=k(1500-P)$, so $P(t)=1500-Ce^{-kt}$. From $P(0)=300$, $C=1200$. Using $P(10)=500$ gives $e^{-10k}=1000/1200=5/6$, hence $k=\\ln(6/5)/10$. To reach $800$ students, $e^{-kt}=(1500-800)/1200=7/12$. Therefore $t=\\frac{10\\ln(12/7)}{\\ln(6/5)}\\approx29.563$ days, or $30$ days after the start."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a1fe324-69f6-5f4f-ae16-55a872bd809c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1005$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122933} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0a5949-da89-513e-b03f-ddb7fa6aa7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122934} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa71735-2ec4-5cf6-8b7a-35bcbcf4c36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$850$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122935} {:answer/id #uuid "1c80e5d3-c034-55b8-81b4-61017785fef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$942$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122936} {:answer/id #uuid "50eb4fa4-b748-5dfe-8015-56d79f9b0e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$986$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf0a5949-da89-513e-b03f-ddb7fa6aa7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122934} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81c5408-efc5-5c6c-9065-f0d086ce6d1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d1a929a-56b1-5075-bd38-17c32dd77741" :question/math-academy-id "q-37400" :question/problem "The population of students $P(t)$ in a school can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.016(2500 - P)$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in years. If the initial population of the school is $800$ students, calculate the population after $4$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Separating the equation gives $P(t)=2500-Ce^{-0.016t}$. Since $P(0)=800$, $C=1700$. Therefore $P(4)=2500-1700e^{-0.064}\\approx905.392$, so the population rounds to $905$ students."}]}
